<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  §6.3's "Move to" sheet: the panel a Material alert dialog puts under its own title, and the list the reader
  presses.

  This file exists because every other list in this app is a `RecyclerView` fed by a `ListAdapter`
  (`ui/tasks/TasksAdapter`, the Stage 15 keep preview) and the sheet used to inflate one four-view row per stored
  destination into a `LinearLayout` inside a `ScrollView`, on the main thread, before the dialog was even shown.
  A destination list is as long as the profile: the §14 10k case is ten thousand rows built for a choice between
  them, and a reader who opens the sheet to move one task should not pay for the nine thousand they will never see.
  The list view is the app's own answer to that, with `MoveDestinationAdapter` diffing the rows.

  **The padding belongs here, not in code.** The panel AppCompat hands a custom view (`@id/customPanel`) carries
  no padding of its own: the dialog's title and message get theirs from the layouts that live beside them. The
  code-built panel this file replaces therefore drew its rows flush against the dialog's edge — the finding 1B was
  written to close — so the horizontal inset is the house's `screen_margin`, the same token every list row on every
  fragment uses, and the vertical inset is the scale's own `spacing_small`/`spacing_normal`. No literal, because
  `dimens.xml` says the scale exists so that no layout needs one again.

  **`wrap_content` on the list is what makes the recycling real, not what defeats it.** The dialog measures this
  panel against the window's own height, so the list is bounded by what is left of the screen and
  `LinearLayoutManager` binds only the rows that fit — which is the whole reason it replaces a `ScrollView` around
  a stack of views. How many rows that is on a given handset, whether the Material scroll indicators appear, and
  whether the sheet ends up taller than the window on a long topic list are frames for §11's device matrix; the
  JVM test measures the same property directly (`HierarchyMoveSheetTest` bounds a large list and counts bound
  children against the adapter's), because Robolectric gives box arithmetic and no window constraints to inherit.

  The header says which task is being moved and where it sits now — `move_current_path_format`, the same sentence
  the confirmation repeats — because a destination list is only half the question «куда я это перенесу»; the other
  half is «откуда», and the reader should not have to remember it from the screen behind the dialog. The dialog's
  own title stays `move_to_title`, which is what TalkBack reads as the heading (§6.3's design decision 6).
-->
<LinearLayout xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
    android:orientation="vertical"
    android:paddingStart="@dimen/screen_margin"
    android:paddingEnd="@dimen/screen_margin"
    android:paddingTop="@dimen/spacing_small"
    android:paddingBottom="@dimen/spacing_normal">

    <!-- The task the editor is holding: the row whose branch every destination below would carry. -->
    <TextView
        android:id="@+id/moveSheetHeadTitle"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:ellipsize="end"
        android:maxLines="2"
        android:textAppearance="@style/TextAppearance.TaskPlanner.Body"
        tools:text="draft" />

    <!-- Where it sits now, in the app's own sentence. Wrapped rather than ellipsized: a path cut off is a place the
         reader cannot check, and §6.3 asks the current path to be seen before the press. -->
    <TextView
        android:id="@+id/moveSheetCurrentPath"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:layout_marginTop="@dimen/spacing_xs"
        android:textAppearance="@style/TextAppearance.TaskPlanner.Caption"
        android:textColor="?attr/colorOnSurfaceVariant"
        tools:text="Now: office-block → planning → draft" />

    <!-- The destinations, one row each, drawn by `MoveDestinationAdapter` from `item_move_destination.xml` in the
         policy's own order. `scrollbars` rather than a `ScrollView` wrapper: the list is the scroller. -->
    <androidx.recyclerview.widget.RecyclerView
        android:id="@+id/moveDestinationList"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:layout_marginTop="@dimen/spacing_small"
        android:scrollbars="vertical"
        tools:itemCount="3"
        tools:listitem="@layout/item_move_destination" />
</LinearLayout>
